  "candidate_0031": {
    "reason": "The assertion that RAG 'involves' a retriever searching an external store is factually true for standard implementations but not universally guaranteed; some systems use internal memory or hybrid retrieval without distinct 'external' stores. However, the definition of RAG inherently implies this step in current architectural norms.",
    "counterexample": "A system using only pre-computed, static knowledge graphs without real-time external document search still performs retrieval-augmented generation by augmenting context, challenging the necessity of an 'external document store' as a strict condition.",
    "sound_as_stated": true
  },
  "candidate_0032": {
    "reason": "The claim that the retriever 'selects' documents to form a context set is functionally accurate for most RAG pipelines, but alternative implementations exist where the retriever ranks without explicit selection (e.g., passing top-k scores directly to a reranker or using dense retrieval embeddings without discrete document selection).",
    "counterexample": "A system that retrieves raw embedding vectors and passes them to a downstream model without forming a discrete 'context set' of text documents challenges the sufficiency of the 'selects...to form a context set' predicate.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  },
  "candidate_0033": {
    "reason": "The assertion that the context set is 'injected' alongside the query into the transformer input is generally true for standard RAG, but alternative architectures (e.g., separate processing branches or late-fusion attention mechanisms) may not inject them into a single unified input sequence in the manner described.",
    "counterexample": "A system that processes the retrieved context and query through parallel transformer streams before merging outputs challenges the specific mechanism of 'injecting...into the transformer's input sequence' as a singular, necessary step.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  }
}
